      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. **Primary request and intent**\n   - Solve GitHub issue https://github.com/linksplatform/Comparisons.SQLiteVSDoublets/issues/107.\n   - Working directory: /tmp/gh-issue-solver-1791131286882. Branch: issue-107-bce58c21006b. Draft PR #108 already exists; update it with `gh pr edit` and do not create a new PR.\n   - Push only to issue-107-bce58c21006b. Make atomic commits. Run local checks before pushing.\n   - When finished: merge main into the branch if needed, make sure CI passes, update the PR description, then run `gh pr ready 108`.\n   - Put experiment scripts in ./experiments and usage examples in ./examples.\n   - Git user is konard. The user's email may be used only for authorship and must never be sent to unrelated services.\n\n   **Issue #107 requirements** (the issue has no comments):\n   - Split the benchmarks into two top-level categories, and generate the README results in exactly this hierarchy:\n     ```\n     Doublets vs SQLite as storage for links\n       Rust doublets vs SQLite\n         32 bit address/id space benchmarks\n         64 bit address/id space benchmarks\n       C# doublets vs SQLite\n         32 bit address/id space benchmarks\n         64 bit address/id space benchmarks\n     Doublets vs SQLite as storage for objects\n       (same Rust/C# × 32/64-bit structure)\n     ```\n   - The SQLite links table must be `links(id, from, to)`, with id typed as int or bigint consistently between C# and Rust, and with obvious indices on id, from and to.\n   - Coverage must be fully symmetric across languages and bit widths.\n   - Both Rust and C# need separate object-storage benchmarks with a sequences cache and without one.\n   - Update every dependency to its latest version.\n   - Code should be self-explanatory: few comments, good names, short and concise, using the most common ways to use SQLite and Doublets in each language.\n   - No false positives or false negatives in the benchmarks.\n   - Fit the largest data sizes possible (100k, 1M, 10M, 100M) into a 6-hour CI budget, splitting CI jobs per benchmark if needed. Use fewer repetitions at larger sizes so the total work is roughly constant, following benchmarking best practices.\n   - \"Make it perfect.\"\n\n2. **Key technical concepts**\n   - Rust:\n     - Criterion 0.3.6 with `iter_custom`; preparation runs outside the measured region through the Benched/Fork pattern.\n     - rusqlite 0.27 (bundled).\n     - doublets-rs git rev 5522d91c…, using `unit::Store` / `split::Store` with `Alloc<…, Global>` or `FileMapped`.\n     - Pinned toolchain nightly-2022-08-22 (needed for `#![feature(allocator_api)]`); many dev-dependencies are pinned for rustc 1.65 compatibility.\n     - The link type is `usize` only, i.e. 64-bit on CI.\n   - C#:\n     - .NET 8 (global.json pins 8.0.100 with rollForward latestFeature), BenchmarkDotNet 0.13.6, EF Core Sqlite 7.0.9.\n     - Platform.Data.Doublets 0.17.2, Platform.Data.Doublets.Sequences 0.5.2, Platform.Diagnostics 0.1.0.\n     - The links benchmark uses `uint` only (32-bit).\n     - The old object benchmark (`Benchmarks.cs`) uses EF Core against DoubletsDbContext (split, file-mapped, `uint`), with a `CachingConverterDecorator` for strings.\n   - The six storage variants are: SQLite_Memory, SQLite_File, Doublets_United_Volatile, Doublets_United_NonVolatile, Doublets_Split_Volatile, Doublets_Split_NonVolatile.\n   - Link operations: create, update, delete, query_all (\"Each All\"), query_by_id, query_by_source_target (\"Concrete\"), query_by_source (\"Outgoing\"), query_by_target (\"Incoming\").\n   - Object operations: objects_create, objects_read, objects_delete. These currently run in Rust only.\n   - Reporting: `scripts/benchmark_report.py` holds the shared code (tables, speedup relative to the fastest SQLite variant, linear and log charts, README marker replacement). `rust/out.py` parses Criterion bencher output; `csharp/out.py` parses the BenchmarkDotNet full JSON. README markers are `<!--RUST_BENCHMARK_RESULTS_START/END-->` and `<!--CSHARP_BENCHMARK_RESULTS_START/END-->`, in both README.md and README.ru.md.\n\n3. **Files and code sections (all read, none modified)**\n   - **README.md / README.ru.md**\n     - Contain the \"Automated benchmark suite\" description, the local run commands, and the C# and Rust result sections between the markers.\n     - Below those sections sit the historical C# object comparison code and results.\n     - Current published sizes: 1000 benchmarked links, 3000 background links, 1000 objects.\n     - Doublets objects are currently 6–75x slower than SQLite in Rust.\n   - **.github/workflows/rust.yml**\n     - Jobs:\n       - `test`: matrix of ubuntu, macos and windows; runs fmt, clippy `-D warnings`, `cargo test --release --lib --tests`, and `cargo check --release --benches`.\n       - `results-pipeline`: Python unittest.\n       - `benchmark-pr`: reduced scale with LINK 10, BG 30, OBJ 5, sample 10, measurement 1s, warm-up 100ms.\n       - `benchmark` (full): main only, timeout 180, LINK 1000, BG 3000, OBJ 1000, sample 20, 3s measurement, 1000ms warm-up. It runs `out.py --readme ../README.md --readme ../README.ru.md --docs-dir ../docs/benchmarks`, then commits \"Update Rust benchmark results [skip ci]\" as LinksPlatformBencher (linksplatform@gmail.com).\n     - Env sets RUST_TOOLCHAIN to nightly-2022-08-22.\n   - **.github/workflows/CI.yml (C#)**\n     - Same structure: setup-dotnet 8.0.x; `dotnet build -c Release --warnaserror`; `--self-test`.\n     - Benchmarks run with `--filter '*LinksBenchmarks*'`, using BENCHMARK_WARMUP_COUNT and BENCHMARK_ITERATION_COUNT (1/1 on PRs, 2/5 on full runs).\n     - The report is found via `find BenchmarkDotNet.Artifacts/results -name '*LinksBenchmarks-report-full.json'`.\n   - **Other workflow and config files**\n     - cpp.yml is legacy (conan; cpp/ contains only an empty conanfile.txt).\n     - AutoMerge.yml covers dependabot merges.\n     - dependabot.yml covers nuget only.\n   - **Recent CI timings** for the last full runs on main:\n     - C# full benchmark took about 18 minutes (run 35459522587).\n     - Rust full benchmark took about 32 minutes (run 35459522519).\n     - Test jobs take 1–5 minutes.\n   - **rust/Cargo.toml**\n     - Dependencies: `rusqlite = { version = \"=0.27.0\", features = [\"bundled\"] }`; doublets from git rev 5522d91c536654934b7181829f6efb570fb8bb44; once_cell 1.14.\n     - Dev-dependencies: criterion =0.3.6 plus many pins.\n     - Release profile: lto, codegen-units 1.\n   - **rust/src/lib.rs**\n     - Defines `BENCHMARK_LINK_COUNT` (Lazy env, default 1000), `BACKGROUND_LINK_COUNT` (default 3000), `temp_path`, and `Link{id,source,target: u64}`.\n     - The `Links` trait has: create, create_point, update, delete, delete_all, query_all, query_by_id, query_by_source, query_by_target, query_by_source_target, count.\n     - `fill_background(links, count) -> Vec<u64>` creates point links.\n     - `fill_benchmarked(links, background, count) -> Vec<Link>` sets source to `bg[i%len]` and target to `bg[(i%len+1+i/len)%len]`.\n     - A `bench!` macro is defined but unused.\n   - **rust/src/fork.rs, exclusive.rs, benched/{mod,sqlite_benched,doublets_benched}.rs**\n     - Contain a lot of boilerplate.\n     - The `Benched` trait has setup(Builder), fork() -> Fork, and `unsafe unfork`.\n     - SQLite unfork calls `reset()`, which drops and recreates the tables. Doublets unfork calls `delete_all()`.\n     - The NonVolatile variants delete their temp files on Drop.\n     - `Exclusive` appears unused.\n   - **rust/src/sqlite_impl.rs**\n     - Schema: `links(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, source, target)`, indexes idx_source, idx_target and idx_source_target, plus `blog_posts(id, title, content, publication_date_time INTEGER)` with a unique title index.\n     - Each create or update is an individual autocommit `execute`, which is very slow on a file database because it fsyncs per row. Statements are prepared on every call rather than cached.\n     - `Objects` inserts inside a transaction.\n   - **rust/src/doublets_impl.rs**\n     - Defines the type aliases with `T = usize`.\n     - `DoubletsLinks<S>` holds a store plus an `ObjectsState` with markers and a symbol↔char HashMap. That HashMap is itself a sequence cache.\n     - Strings are stored as a left-fold of (prev, symbol) links via `get_or_create`.\n     - `delete_all` iterates over all links and deletes them.\n     - Objects are blog_post links of the form `(marker, self)`; properties are `(object, marker)` → `(property, value)`.\n     - `create` uses `store.create_link(source, target)`.\n   - **rust/src/objects.rs**\n     - `BENCHMARK_OBJECT_COUNT` (default 1000), xorshift `generate_posts`, `CONTENTS` holding 5 shortened lorem paragraphs, `BlogPost{id,title,content,publication_date_time:i64}`.\n     - The `Objects` trait has create_posts, read_posts, delete_posts, count_posts.\n   - **rust/benches/**\n     - bench.rs is a criterion_group of 11 targets.\n     - benchmarks/mod.rs has `measure` (iter_custom with prepare outside timing), the `benchmark_subject!` and `benchmark_operation!` macros (6 subjects each), and `configure_criterion` with env BENCHMARK_SAMPLE_SIZE, BENCHMARK_MEASUREMENT_SECONDS and BENCHMARK_WARM_UP_MILLISECONDS.\n     - links.rs: create, update (uses new update_sources so pairs are unique), delete.\n     - each.rs: `query_benchmark!` macro.\n     - objects.rs: create, read, delete.\n   - **rust/tests/storages.rs**\n     - Runs check_links, check_objects and check_objects_and_links for all 6 subjects via `storage_tests!`.\n   - **rust/examples/inspect_storage.rs**: an inspection helper.\n   - **csharp/Comparisons.SQLiteVSDoublets.csproj**\n     - net8.0, Nullable enabled, NoWarn NETSDK1206.\n     - Conditional Debug ProjectReference to a local Data.Doublets.Sequences checkout.\n   - **csharp/Program.cs**\n     - `--self-test` runs LinksSelfTest.\n     - `--manual-object-test` exists.\n     - With no args it runs the legacy `BenchmarkRunner.Run<Benchmarks>()`.\n     - Otherwise it uses BenchmarkSwitcher with a config built from BENCHMARK_WARMUP_COUNT and BENCHMARK_ITERATION_COUNT, a full JSON exporter, and `EnvironmentValue`.\n   - **csharp/LinksBenchmarks.cs**\n     - `[ParamsAllValues] LinksVariant Variant`, plus N from BENCHMARK_LINK_COUNT.\n     - IterationSetup per target creates the storage and fills background (BACKGROUND_LINK_COUNT) and benchmarked links; IterationCleanup disposes.\n     - Methods: Create, Update, Delete, EachAll, EachIdentity, EachConcrete, EachOutgoing, EachIncoming.\n     - Each method runs a single invocation per iteration, which BenchmarkDotNet warns about for short methods.\n   - **csharp/Links/**\n     - `ILinksStorage` (uint API).\n     - `LinkRecord(uint Id, uint Source, uint Target)` is a record struct.\n     - `LinksData.FillBackground` / `FillBenchmarked` use the same formula as Rust.\n     - `LinksStorageFactory` defines the `LinksVariant` enum and creates `UnitedMemoryLinks<uint>` / `SplitMemoryLinks<uint>` with HeapResizableDirectMemory or file paths in a temp dir.\n     - `DoubletsLinksStorage` uses CreateAndUpdate, `_links.All(...)`, and `Count => QueryAll().Count`.\n     - `SQLiteLinksStorage` uses Microsoft.Data.Sqlite with Pooling=false and the same schema; commands are created per call without transactions.\n   - **csharp/LinksSelfTest.cs**: checks every variant.\n   - **Legacy C# object benchmark files**\n     - Benchmarks.cs, TestRun.cs, TestRunResults.cs, SizeAfterCreationColumn.cs, SQLite/SQLiteDbContext.cs (EF Core, table \"BlogPosts\" in schema \"test\"), SQLite/SQLiteTestRun.cs, Doublets/DoubletsDbContext.cs, Doublets/DoubletsTestRun.cs, Model/BlogPost.cs, Model/BlogPosts.cs (random data, full-length lorem paragraphs).\n     - TestRun.cs has heavy boilerplate XML doc comments.\n   - **Reporting scripts and outputs**\n     - csharp/out.py maps METHODS to operation keys and uses LINK_OPERATIONS only, with `object_count=False`.\n     - Tests live in scripts/test_benchmark_report.py, rust/test_out.py and csharp/test_out.py.\n     - docs/benchmarks/ holds bench_csharp(.png, _log_scale.png) and bench_rust(.png, _log_scale.png).\n     - rust/results.md and csharp/results.md are generated.\n   - **.gitkeep**: auto-generated for PR creation. It can be removed when finalizing if appropriate.\n\n4. **Errors and fixes**\n   - No errors yet. Locally, matplotlib/numpy are not installed (pip install is needed for the Python tests).\n\n5. **Problem solving and observations**\n   - The local environment has cargo/rustup with stable only (the pinned nightly must be installed), dotnet SDK 10.0.112 only (no 8.0), python3 without matplotlib, 6 CPUs, 11 GB RAM, and 139 GB free disk.\n   - Gaps against the issue:\n     - Rust is 64-bit only and C# links are 32-bit only.\n     - C# has no object benchmark in the new pipeline; it only has the legacy one.\n     - There is no sequences cache on/off split.\n     - SQLite columns are named source/target, but the issue asks for from/to. `from` is an SQL keyword and must be quoted, or the columns could be named `\"from\"`/`\"to\"`.\n     - Dependencies are old: criterion 0.3 to 0.5+, rusqlite to the latest, BenchmarkDotNet to the latest, EF Core / Microsoft.Data.Sqlite to the latest, Platform.Data.Doublets to the latest. The .NET target may move to 8/9/10.\n     - Sizes are tiny (1000 items); the issue wants 100k–100M with a scaled number of repetitions, within 6 hours, possibly as one CI job per benchmark.\n     - The README needs restructuring into the hierarchy above.\n     - There is a lot of boilerplate (Benched/Fork/Exclusive, the unused bench! macro, doc-comment noise).\n   - Possible false results:\n     - SQLite per-row autocommit on a file database versus a Doublets mmap without fsync.\n     - The C# SQLite object path uses EF Core while Doublets uses direct calls.\n     - The Rust Doublets objects use a HashMap char cache.\n     - Doublets `Count` in C# calls QueryAll.\n\n6. **All user messages**\n   - \"Issue to solve: https://github.com/linksplatform/Comparisons.SQLiteVSDoublets/issues/107 / Your prepared branch: issue-107-bce58c21006b / Your prepared working directory: /tmp/gh-issue-solver-1791131286882 / Your prepared Pull Request: https://github.com/linksplatform/Comparisons.SQLiteVSDoublets/pull/108 / Proceed.\"\n   - The system-provided context also states that the user's email (link.assistant.team@proton.me) is for identification and authorship only and must never be sent to unrelated services.\n\n7. **Pending tasks** (all of the issue's requirements)\n   - Restructure both languages so the links and objects categories each run at 32-bit and 64-bit address widths (Rust: u32/u64 doublets stores and SQLite; C#: uint/ulong).\n   - Use an SQLite table `links(id, from, to)` with indices.\n   - Add C# object benchmarks to the new pipeline, and add object benchmarks with and without a sequences cache in both languages.\n   - Update all dependencies to their latest versions, which may require moving off nightly-2022-08-22 and checking the doublets-rs latest rev / crates.io version.\n   - Simplify the code and reduce redundant comments.\n   - Choose scaled sizes (100k/1M/10M/100M) with fewer repetitions for larger sizes; split CI per benchmark if needed within 6-hour job limits; research best practices.\n   - Generate the README (EN and RU) in exactly the requested hierarchy; update the reporting scripts and Python tests.\n   - Add tests that verify correctness, so there are no false positives or negatives.\n   - Run local checks: cargo fmt, clippy, tests; dotnet build/self-test (installing the .NET 8 SDK or retargeting); Python tests.\n   - Commit, push, verify CI, update the PR #108 title and description in the style of PR #106, then run `gh pr ready 108`.\n\n8. **Current work**\n 